nodes.statement:
The proof is similar to Proposition 10.3 and Proposition 10.4 in ~\cite{FH}. We omit the proof but stress the differences. First of all, we need to use Theorem \ref{nU3s} to decompose $\chi_{N}$ into the sum $\chi_{N,s}+\chi_{N,u}+\chi_{N,e}$. Then we can expand the left hand side of (\ref{temp9}) into 81 terms. By a similar argument as Proposition 10.5 in ~\cite{FH}, we have that
   \begin{equation}\nonumber
  \begin{split}
    \liminf_{N\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}_{(\alpha,\beta)\in\Theta_{N}}\int_{\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{Z}[i]}}{\chi_{N,s}(\alpha+\gamma_{1}\beta)\chi_{N,s}(\alpha+\gamma_{2}\beta)\overline{\chi_{N,s}}(\alpha+\gamma_{3}\beta)
    \overline{\chi_{N,s}}(\alpha+\gamma_{4}\beta)}d\nu(\chi)
  \end{split}
  \end{equation}
is bounded below by a positive number which is independent of $\epsilon$. So
 it suffices to show that all other terms are negligible. A term is obvious $O(\epsilon)$ if it contents the expression $\chi_{N,e}$. It then suffices to show that all terms containing the expression $\chi_{N,u}$ are negligible, which holds immediately if one can show that
      \begin{equation} \begin{split}
      \Bigl\vert\mathbb{E}_{\alpha,\beta\in R_{\tilde{N}}}\bold{1}_{R_{N}}(\beta)\prod_{j=0}^{3}a_{j}(\alpha+\gamma_{j}\beta)\Bigr\vert
      \leq C\min_{1\leq j\leq 4}(\Vert a_{j}\Vert_{U^{3}(R_{\tilde{N}})})^{1/3}+\frac{10}{\tilde{N}}
    \end{split}
  \end{equation}
for all functions $a_{1},\dots,a_{4}$ on $R_{\tilde{N}}$ with $\Vert a_{j}\Vert_{L^{\infty}(R_{\tilde{N}})}\leq 1$ for $j=1,\dots,4$, where $C>0$ depends only on $\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{4}$ (the exponent on the right hand side of (\ref{temp8}) is $\frac{1}{2}$ for the 1-dimensional case and is $\frac{1}{3}$ for the 2-dimensional case). The proof of it is a straightforward generalization of Lemma 10.7 in ~\cite{FH} and Theorem 2.1 in ~\cite{FH} in the 2-dimensional case, and so we are done.

conclusions:
there exist an action by dilation $\{T_{\alpha}\}_{\alpha\in\mathbb{Z}[i]}$ on a probability space $(X,\mathcal{B},\mu)$ and a set $A\in\mathcal{B}$ with $\mu(A)=d_{mult}(E)$ such that for all $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and for all $\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k}\in\mathbb{Z}[i]$, we have
    \begin{equation}\nonumber
  \begin{split}
    d_{mult}(\alpha_{1}^{-1}E\cap\dots\cap \alpha_{k}^{-1}E)\geq\mu(T_{\alpha_{1}}^{-1}A\cap\dots\cap T_{\alpha_{k}}^{-1}A),
  \end{split}
  \end{equation}

nodes.statement:
For every $f\in L^{2}(\mu)$, by the spectral theorem, there exists a positive finite measure $\nu$ (called the \emph{spectral measure  of $f$}) on the group of
multiplicative functions $\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{Z}[i]}$ such that for all $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{Z}[i]$,
\begin{equation}\nonumber
  \begin{split}
    \int_{X}{T_{\alpha}f\cdot T_{\beta}\overline{f}}d\mu=\int_{X}{T_{\alpha/\beta}f\cdot \overline{f}}d\mu=\int_{\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{Z}[i]}}{\chi(\alpha/\beta)}d\nu(\chi)
    =\int_{\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{Z}[i]}}{\chi(\alpha)\overline{\chi}(\beta)}d\nu(\chi).
  \end{split}
  \end{equation}

conclusions:
for every $A\in\mathcal{B}$ with $\mu(A)>0$, there exist $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{Z}[i]$ such that $(\alpha+\gamma_{1}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{2}\beta)$ and $(\alpha+\gamma_{3}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{4}\beta)$ are distinct and non-zero, and
    \begin{equation}\nonumber
  \begin{split}
    \mu(T_{(\alpha+\gamma_{1}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{2}\beta)}^{-1}A\cap T_{(\alpha+\gamma_{3}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{4}\beta)}^{-1}A)>0.
  \end{split}
  \end{equation}

\liminf_{N\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}_{(\alpha,\beta)\in\Theta_{N}}\mu(T_{(\alpha+\gamma_{1}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{2}\beta)}^{-1}A\cap T_{(\alpha+\gamma_{3}\beta)(\alpha+\gamma_{4}\beta)}^{-1}A)>0,
  \end{split}
  \end{equation}
  where $\Theta_{N}=\{(\alpha,\beta)\in R_{N}\times  R_{N}\colon \alpha+\gamma_{i}\beta\in R_{N}, i=1,2,3\}$

