to $H^{\underline{ old{k}}}$ because $ eta_{i}=[\lambda,\alpha'_{i}]v_{i}\alpha'_{i}$ and $[\lambda,\alpha'_{i}]v_{i}\in H_{2}\subset H^{\underline{ old{k}}}$. Also $ eta_{d}=[\lambda,v_{d}]v_{d}\in H_{2}\subset H^{\underline{ old{k}}}$. Therefore, $g_{2}(n)$ is a polynomial sequence in $H^{\underline{ old{k}}}$ having the form as stated. Note that in the special case when $H_{2}=H_{2}^{\underline{ old{k}}}$, we have that $ eta_{i}\in H_{2}^{\underline{ old{k}}}$ for all $1\leq i\leq d$. So Property 3) is satisfied. By (ef{temp51}) and (ef{temp61}), we have that  egin{equation}
onumber  egin{split} g_{1}(p\cdot d!n+k_{0})\cdot e_{Y}=\Bigl(\prod_{i=1}^{d-1}\omega_{i}^{p_{i}(n)}\Bigr)h_{0}'g_{2}(n)\cdot e_{Y}. \end{split} \end{equation} For $n\leq 2L$, by the right invariance of the metric $d_{H}$, we have that  egin{equation}
onumber  egin{split} d_{H}(\prod_{i=1}^{d-1}\omega_{i}^{p_{i}(n)},id_{H})\leq \sum_{i=1}^{d-1}KC_{2}L^{i}/N^{i}\leq dKC_{2}L/N\leq\kappa/2. \end{split} \end{equation} So  egin{equation}
onumber  egin{split} d_{Y}\Bigl(g_{1}(p \cdot d!n+k_{0})\cdot e_{Y},h_{0}'g_{2}(n)\cdot e_{Y}\Bigr)\leq\kappa/2 \end{split} \end{equation} Since $\Vert f\Vert_{Lip(X)}\leq 1$, it follows from (ef{temp71}) that  egin{equation}
onumber  egin{split} &\quad\Bigl\vert\mathbb{E}_{n\in[N]} old{1}_{P'}(n)F(g_{2}(n)\cdot e_{Y})\Bigr\vert =\Bigl\vert\mathbb{E}_{n\in[N]} old{1}_{P'}(n)f(h_{0}'g_{2}(n)\cdot e_{Y})\Bigr\vert \&\geq \Bigl\vert\mathbb{E}_{n\in[N]} old{1}_{P'}(n)f(g_{1}(p \cdot d!n+k_{0})\cdot e_{Y})\Bigr\vert-\kappa/2>\kappa/2, \end{split} \end{equation} and $F$ has a bounded Lipschitz constant since $h_{0}'\in F_{0}$. Moreover, by assumption, $f|_{H^{\underline{ old{k}}}}$ is of $S$-frequency and thus so is $F|_{H^{\underline{ old{k}}}}$, which proves Property 1). We are left with proving Property 2). Note that $ eta_{1}=v'\alpha'_{1}$ for some $v'\in H_{2}$, which implies that $ old{p}\cdot	ilde{v'}=0$ since $ old{p}$ is of the same type as $H$. Therefore,  egin{equation}
onumber  egin{split} \Vert p old{p}\cdot	ilde{\alpha_{1}}\Vert_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}}= \Vert old{p}\cdot	ilde{(\omega_{1} eta_{1})}\Vert_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} \leq \Vert old{p}\cdot	ilde{\omega_{1}}\Vert_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} +\Vert old{p}\cdot	ilde{ eta_{1}}\Vert_{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} \leq rac{(C_{2}+1)D}{N}. \end{split} \end{equation}

Since $\bold{k}$ is of the same type as $H$, it is easy to see that $H_{2}\subset H^{\underline{\bold{k}}}$.

In the proof, $C_{1},C_{2}$ are constants depending only on $X,C,\kappa$ and $d$. 
  We assume without loss of generality that $g_{1}(n)$ has no constant term, i.e. we assume $g_{1}(n)=\prod_{i=1}^{d}\alpha_{i}^{\binom{n}{i}}$.
  Let $F_{0}\subset H$ be a bounded fundamental domain of the projection $H\rightarrow Y$ (we assume $F_{0}$ is fixed given $Y$). Then by Lemma \ref{cts}, there exists $C_{1}>0$ such that
   \begin{equation}\nonumber
   \begin{split}
